      "math_academy_id": "q-51443",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3f41b2bd-0c1d-5267-b91d-7aba35ea7efd",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function From Its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/7aff74d99be667ea7b51bfcfaee2c0e701e3a553656aa931ba3f1f1ba0115ffb.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [0,1]$ and the piecewise linear for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-{A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
