    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-49205",
      "knowledge_point_id": "75f1ac34-ae96-53af-b8ae-d465af28f75d",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Nonlinear Function From Its Graph",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14007648/assets/5d6f61767434332bbb92574ff87d72bda4b07028d77bda5925f73b32a7c751d5.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(2,-2)$ with radius $2.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-2,0]$ and the arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [0,4].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,4].$ The corresponding region is a combination of a semi-circle and a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2}+lw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\pi +8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{3}=-2\\pi -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}-{A}_{3} \\\\  & =2-2-2\\pi -8 \\\\  & =-2\\pi -8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
